{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh81LPPB2veoVrZH2gV3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I renamed the game file to use the full title and source names, and added a browser audit that checks the controller, keyboard and mouse requirements from TASK.md. All 165 checks now pass, including that audit and the real AI Hub harness.\n\n**What changed**\n- **File name:** the game is now `Bentou2BirthOfTheLunchboxAssassins-Tenchu2BirthOfTheStealthAssassins-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-183420.html`, and the dev folder has the same name. This matches the `GameTitle-SOURCE-…` CamelCase style the other runs here use; the earlier name abbreviated both titles.\n- **Browser audit (`tests/browser/audit.mjs`):** it runs the real bundle with a simulated DualSense (a stub controller, not real hardware), keyboard and mouse:\n  - Button prompts switch between PlayStation symbols, the Xbox setting and keys.\n  - The d-pad and ✕ drive the menus; the left stick moves the ninja and □ strikes.\n  - The mouse wheel cycles tools.\n  - Options opens the pause menu, which has Dev Mode; hovering moves the focus there.\n  - A balance slider changes the live value, and reset restores the default.\n  - In the Workshop, the mouse paints a wall, Esc opens the menu, and Export shows the level JSON.\n- **Bugs the audit found, now fixed:**\n  - After using a gamepad, the mouse never took over again, so the Workshop couldn't paint.\n  - The Workshop missed quick key taps.\n  - The mouse wheel was ignored during an attack.\n- **Feature list (`FEATURES.md`):** each TASK.md requirement, with where it's built and which test covers it.\n\nReal controller hardware is still untested. The About pages and `exit-interview.json` (which still parses) were updated with the new test count and issues.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:34:20\n- **Source:** Tenchu 2: Birth of the Stealth Assassins (Acquire / Activision, PlayStation, 2000)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Third-person ninja stealth-action with melee combat and boss duels. Isometric third person, turnable in 45° steps and zoomable, with a high three-quarter camera option.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff engine with nine small patches (listed in `engine/PATCHES.md`), AI Hub session client 1.4.0 (unmodified), plain JavaScript in one HTML file. Tests use Node 24 and Playwright 1.63 with Chromium.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native PS1-era pixel art (320×240, expanded to fill the screen). Detailed character models with new masks, hats and weapons, canvas lighting, and Japanese props and materials written as shape data.\n- **Animation:** The engine's procedural character animation, plus new Silent Cut moves (5 kinds × 2 characters), grapple, riposte, ninja run, wall hug, swimming, eating, and a four-legged hound.\n- **Audio:** The engine's chip synth. Layered Japanese-scale music (koto, shamisen, shakuhachi, fue, taiko), sound effects made in code, and a gibberish voice synth for every spoken line.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode, 1–4 players, with the game's own pre-round card. Offline, the Shadow Trial lets you race your own best run.\n- **Testing:** One command runs everything and prints only failures and a one-line count; 165 checks pass.\n  - Headless suites cover the mechanics, all four missions completed through their objectives (an automated player fights each boss), menus, the story and ending, attract mode, Dev Mode, the Workshop, a mock AI Hub, and determinism.\n  - Browser checks cover the bundle, the audit with a simulated DualSense, keyboard and mouse, and the real AI Hub harness (2 players; 4 players on a lossy network; the host leaving).\n- **Research:** The engine handoff docs and the AI Hub README, plus the planner's research (Wikipedia on Tenchu 2) carried in PLAN.md.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 12 real issues, all fixed:\n   - Guards counted the hero's visibility twice, so they barely noticed anyone.\n   - The attack from above never triggered from a roof edge.\n   - Diving was impossible: the engine's movement code undid the crouch toggle.\n   - Guards got stuck on solid props.\n   - A held button didn't count as activity, so attract mode could start while it was held.\n   - Test teleports left the camera behind during slow motion.\n   - The mouse never took over again after a gamepad was used.\n   - The Workshop missed quick key taps.\n   - The mouse wheel was ignored during an attack.\n   - Plus three test-setup mistakes: rice bales in a path, a hound that smelled the tester, and Python buffering the local server's port number.\n2. **How many were critical or game-breaking?** 3: no diving, guards stuck on props, and the double-counted visibility.\n3. **Most helpful test:** The missions suite. It completes all four missions through their real objectives, with the automated player fighting each boss.\n4. **Least helpful test:** The headless smoke test with random input. It passed at once because headless runs skip all drawing.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** How much already works headless: parkour movement, sight, suspicion and hearing, paired actions, the built-in pause and settings menus, fixed-step tests, and good-looking characters from little code.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes, a few times:\n   - The movement code treats crouch as a held state, not a toggle.\n   - Pathfinding ignores solid props.\n   - The sight check already includes the target's visibility, so I counted it twice.\n   - A one-step scripted press can be lost during hit-stop.\n   - A custom input map has to restate the menu keys.\n3. **Most important thing I built myself:** The stealth layer: Shadow Sight (unseen guards are hidden) and the Ki gauge, light maps, guard behaviour, Silent Cuts, the bosses, and the mission runtime.\n4. **Hardest part:** Knowing how existing systems behave in a 1.2 MB engine; those details live only in the source.\n5. **What I changed:** Nine small patches:\n   - PlayStation button symbols in the pixel font.\n   - A PlayStation/Xbox setting for button symbols.\n   - Character gear: masks, two hat styles, spear, bow, club, an off-hand blade and a back banner.\n   - Settings rows on custom menu screens.\n   - Mouse hover moves the focus.\n   - Interface scaling for viewing from 10 feet.\n   - Plucked-string music notes.\n   - A volume channel for voices.\n   - Letting the hub keep its Create + Options shortcut while the game is paused.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** Make pathfinding respect solid props.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** It's concrete about the player: a TV at 10 feet, button symbols, attract mode, touch-only devices, dev sliders and the hub. With the detailed plan, little was left to guess.\n2. **What was confusing:** The bracketed file-name pattern versus the plan's own names, and how to weigh \"every feature\" against a plan far larger than one session.\n3. **Hardest thing:** The scope. A full stealth game with four bosses, an escort, a mission editor, attract mode, multiplayer and a dev mode, all tested with no stubs.\n4. **What I'd change:** Mark which plan items are must-haves and which are stretch goals, give a session budget, give one worked example of the file name, and mention the temp-disk limit in TASK.md itself."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1412,"cache_read_input_tokens":652072,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1412},"output_tokens":776,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"65d0dba4-d8b1-427b-bda2-f14280ca26ea","uuid":"30c1334e-b211-47fe-8f67-cac252d66447","timestamp":"2026-11-04T02:45:52.289Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh81L2YfGQ6tcV5op6D4"}
