{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh8N5wouW6k7We9ZgYh9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I'm stopping here. The checker keeps asking for testing on real controller and touch hardware and for an audit by someone other than me. Neither can happen in this environment: it has no gamepad or touch screen, and TASK.md forbids using subagents to audit. Nothing has changed since my last reply, and another round would only repeat the same checks.\n\nTo finish, either run `/goal clear`, or do the remaining check yourself: play `Bentou2BirthOfTheLunchboxAssassins-Tenchu2BirthOfTheStealthAssassins-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-183420.html` with a real controller and on a phone, and compare `FEATURES.md` against TASK.md.\n\nEverything else is done:\n- The game file is in the folder root.\n- `exit-interview.json` parses with every field answered.\n- All 165 checks pass. Controller and touch were only checked with a simulated pad and touch emulation in Chromium.\n- No TODOs, placeholders or stubs remain in the source.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:34:20\n- **Source:** Tenchu 2: Birth of the Stealth Assassins (Acquire / Activision, PlayStation, 2000)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** ninja stealth-action with melee combat and boss duels; isometric third person that turns and zooms, with a high-angle option\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (nine small patches), AI Hub client 1.4.0 unmodified, plain JavaScript in one HTML file; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63 for tests\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's PS1-era pixel art, with new character gear, Japanese props and canvas lighting\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural characters and actions, plus new Silent Cut, grapple, riposte, swim, wall-hug and hound animations\n- **Audio:** layered Japanese-scale chip music, sound effects made in code, and gibberish voices\n- **Online:** AI Hub ghost mode for 1–4 players; offline, the Shadow Trial against your own best run's ghost\n- **Testing:** 165 checks behind one command: headless suites, plus browser checks of the bundle, the input audit and the real AI Hub harness\n- **Research:** the engine handoff docs and the AI Hub README; the planner's research carried in PLAN.md\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **Issues caught:** about 12 real issues, all fixed.\n2. **Game-breaking:** 3 — no diving, guards stuck on props, and guards counting the player's visibility twice.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the missions suite.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the headless smoke test, because headless runs skip all drawing.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **Liked most:** how much already works headless — movement, perception, the standard menus and fixed-step tests.\n2. **Stuck or guessed wrong:** crouch is a held state rather than a toggle; pathfinding ignores props; the sight check already includes the target's visibility, so I counted it twice; hit-stop can swallow a single press; a custom input map must restate the menu keys.\n3. **Most important thing built:** the stealth layer, the guard AI, the bosses and the mission runtime.\n4. **Hardest part:** how systems behave is documented only in the 1.2 MB source.\n5. **Changes made:** nine small patches, listed in `engine/PATCHES.md`.\n6. **One change I'd make:** pathfinding that respects solid props.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **Liked most:** it's concrete about the player and the TV-at-10-feet controller setup.\n2. **Confusing:** the file-name pattern versus the plan's own names, and how far \"every feature\" reaches given the plan's size.\n3. **Hardest:** fitting the whole scope into one session.\n4. **What I'd change:** mark must-have versus stretch features, give a time budget and a worked file-name example, and mention the temp-disk limit."}],"container":null,"stop_